WEBVTT

00:00:00.153 --> 00:00:03.800
A decision is only as strong as the options under consideration.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.760
Many poor decisions start with too few options, often presented as a false binary:

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.285
do this or do nothing, buy the tool or keep suffering,

00:00:10.285 --> 00:00:13.160
centralize everything or decentralize everything.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:14.640
Real choices are often richer.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:18.390
There may be combinations, staged approaches, experiments, temporary controls,

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.200
or partial commitments that fit the situation better than either extreme.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.580
Generating realistic options requires creativity and discipline.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:28.731
Creativity helps the team avoid accepting the first visible answer.

00:00:28.731 --> 00:00:31.715
Discipline keeps the team from inventing options that cannot be executed.

00:00:31.715 --> 00:00:35.120
A useful question is, what are at least three viable paths?

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.540
Another is, what would we do if our preferred option were unavailable?

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:42.296
These prompts force the group to widen the option set without drifting into fantasy.

00:00:42.296 --> 00:00:46.250
Tradeoff analysis asks what each option gains and gives up.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:48.080
Opportunity cost is the value of what is not chosen.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.330
If a team spends two weeks hardening one system,

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.760
those same people cannot spend those two weeks improving another control.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.140
If a leader chooses speed, they may give up some quality or resilience.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.405
If they choose a very robust process, they may give up flexibility or time.

00:01:01.405 --> 00:01:04.750
Decision makers also need to distinguish reversible and irreversible choices.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.759
A reversible choice can be tested, rolled back, or adjusted with modest cost.

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:12.750
An irreversible or hard-to-reverse choice deserves more care because the downside of being

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.040
wrong is larger.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.945
This distinction prevents teams from overanalyzing small experiments and underanalyzing

00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:20.220
commitments that lock in architecture,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:21.505
cost, or risk.

00:01:21.505 --> 00:01:23.330
Sometimes the best decision is not a single big bet.

00:01:23.330 --> 00:01:27.565
It may be a portfolio of options, a sequence of steps, a pilot, a staged rollout,

00:01:27.565 --> 00:01:30.068
or an experiment designed to produce better information.

00:01:30.068 --> 00:01:33.470
Satisficing, or choosing an option that is good enough under the constraints,

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.300
may be wiser than endlessly optimizing when time is scarce and uncertainty is high.
